From 8f5fa1e8d0dd3047c776ebf4d6db57aad6d64316 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stew Forster <stew675@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 08:14:24 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 11/15] rdna-boosts: block 11: skip CUDA graphs for multi-token
 PRE-FILL

The skip must not key off cgraph->nodes[0]->ne[1]: with expert offload
(-ncmoe) the scheduler splits the graph around the CPU-resident experts, so a
one-token decode split starts on an expert tensor [n_ff, n_expert_used, 1]
and ne[1] == n_expert_used > 1.  Every decode split was therefore classified
as pre-fill and decode never replayed a HIP graph.

Read the token count from the first op that carries it instead: MUL_MAT_ID
ne[2], or a weight MUL_MAT's src1->ne[1], with the old nodes[0]->ne[1] test as
the fallback.  On gfx1201 (Qwen3.8-Flash-Next UD-Q4_K_XL, -ncmoe 48,
q8_0 KV) this takes a 16-token decode from 0 graph warmups / 0 replays to
50 / 687, +~20% tg, greedy output byte-identical; dense models and pre-fill
are unchanged.

Because decode now recaptures regularly, ggml_cuda_graph_update_executable()
must also stop using hipGraphExecUpdate on HIP: the driver's
GraphKernelArgManager bump-allocates a kernarg slot on every update and only
reclaims it on hipGraphExecDestroy (ROCm/rocm-systems#10713; driver fix still
an unmerged PR), so a long-lived exec leaks a few KB per token.  Destroy and
re-instantiate on the recapture path instead; GGML_HIP_GRAPH_FORCE_UPDATE=1
keeps the update path available for a ROCm that carries the driver fix.
